  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThrough Tonight: Local radars still showed isolated to scattered \nstorms over the eastern half of the CWA where deeper moisture with \nPWATs greater than an inch and three quarters resided. This activity \nis expected to slowly dissipate by sunrise but additional \ndevelopment is expected this afternoon. latest satellite imagery/RAP \nanalysis showed our CWA with a much weaker ridge aloft overhead and \na closed low still centered over the eastern Gulf near the Florida \npeninsula and a shortwave trough approaching just to our northwest. \n/22/\n\nThe trough moving in from the northwest will replace the lingering \nshortwave ridge overhead this afternoon. Weaker inhibition across \nthe area, and a nearby frontal boundary to our north will help to \ntrigger afternoon convection. Still low-to-moderate (20-40%) POPs, \nbut any location in the area at least has a chance for showers or \nthunderstorms tomorrow. The Marginal Risk for strong to severe \nstorms remains in place across the northern half of the area, with \ndamaging wind gusts being the main hazard. With rain chances \nincreasing, and a remnant of drier air hanging on through the Delta \nareas, the local graphic for heat risk tomorrow was leveled at \n\"Limited\" (1 of 4) for tomorrow. Max heat index values should top \nout near 105 in the hottest areas. /NF/22/\n\nTuesday through Sunday...Weak troughing will exist across mainly far \neastern portions of the forecast area Tuesday, as high pressure \naloft begins building into the region from the west.  At the \nsurface, a frontal boundary will eventually finish dropping south \nthrough the CWA late in the period Tuesday.  This, combined with \ndaytime heating in the middle 90s, will again result in isolated to \nscattered showers and storms developing across areas mainly along \nand southeast of the Natchez Trace Parkway Tuesday afternoon. Heavy \ndownpours and frequent lightning will be possible with storms, with \ngusty winds possible with the most intense storms, particularly \nacross East Mississippi.\n\nWith high pressure aloft and at the surface becoming more prevalent \nacross the region through the remainder of the forecast period, rain \nchances essentially dwindle to nil.  However in the aforementioned \nfrontal boundary's wake, drier air and less hot conditions will \nexist across the forecast area for the remainder of the week and \ninto the weekend.  As highs Wednesday range from the low to middle \n90s, come late week and into the weekend they'll range from the \nupper 80s to lower 90s.  Lows will likewise follow suit as they'll \nrange from the upper 60s and lower 70s Wednesday morning.  Then \ncooling into the low to middle 60s late in the week and into the \nweekend. /19/",
